course: dictionary
lesson: 9774
title: Surge: a sudden rush
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A surge is a sudden, strong rush, like water or people moving forward all at once. «A storm surge is sea water pushed onto the coast by a hurricane's winds.» Here surge means a sudden rise of sea water onto the land.
explain: Surge can also mean a sudden increase. «A surge of anger» means anger that rises up all at once. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: a sudden rush of anger still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Surge can be a noun or a verb. As a verb, to surge means to move forward or rise suddenly and powerfully, so a crowd can surge forward when a gate opens.
fact: Surge can be a noun (a surge) or a verb (to surge, surged, surging).
fact: A surge is a sudden, strong rush, or a sudden increase.
fact: A storm surge is when strong winds push sea water high onto the land, causing floods.
fact: A power surge is a sudden jump in electricity that can damage machines, so people use surge protectors.
fact: Context helps: when you see «storm» or «power» in front of surge, the word tells you what kind of rush it is.
exercise: «The crowd began to surge forward when the gates opened.» What does surge mean here? | To fall asleep | *To move forward suddenly and powerfully | To count slowly | To stand still and wait quietly
exercise: «Before a hurricane, people leave because a storm surge can flood the coast.» What is a storm surge? | A quiet breeze over a field | A sudden jump in electricity | *Strong winds pushing sea water high onto the land
exercise: Mum plugs the computer into a surge protector. Why? | To keep the room warm | *To guard the computer against a sudden jump in electricity | To make the computer look smarter
test: «She felt a surge of pride when her name was called.» What does a surge of pride mean? | A quiet, slow feeling | A feeling of being cold | *A sudden, strong rush of pride
test: «The waves surged over the wall.» Which word is a verb in this sentence? | waves | over | wall | *surged
exam: Which sentence uses surge as a noun? | *The storm brought a huge surge of water onto the road | We surged past the gate | Surging is fun on the trampoline | The water will surge up the beach
